🧬 What is the "Lifespan" of a Dependency?
It refers to how long an object (bean/dependency) stays alive — from when it is created to when it is destroyed.

📦 In Spring (most common in Java), dependencies (beans) have scopes, which define their lifespan.
🔑 Common Spring Bean Scopes (Lifespans):
| Scope                 | Lifespan                             | Description                                           |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| `singleton` (default) | Created once per Spring container    | All requests share the **same instance**              |
| `prototype`           | New object every time it's requested | A **new instance** is created each time it's injected |
| `request`             | One per HTTP request (Web only)      | New instance per **HTTP request**                     |
| `session`             | One per HTTP session (Web only)      | New instance per **user session**                     |
| `application`         | One per `ServletContext`             | Shared across the whole app                           |
| `websocket`           | One per WebSocket session            | For WebSocket apps                                    |


🧪 Example: Singleton vs Prototype
java
Copy
Edit
@Component
@Scope("singleton") // default
class MySingletonBean { }

@Component
@Scope("prototype")
class MyPrototypeBean { }
Usage:
java
Copy
Edit
@Autowired
MySingletonBean a1;
@Autowired
MySingletonBean a2;

// a1 == a2 → true (same instance)

@Autowired
MyPrototypeBean p1;
@Autowired
MyPrototypeBean p2;

// p1 == p2 → false (different instances)
🧠 Summary Table
| Scope       | Managed By                      | Lifetime                                   |
| ----------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Singleton   | Spring Container                | From startup to shutdown                   |
| Prototype   | Your code (Spring just creates) | Until no longer used or manually destroyed |
| Request     | Spring Web Context              | One HTTP request                           |
| Session     | Spring Web Context              | One user session                           |
| Application | Servlet Context                 | Until app stops                            |
| WebSocket   | WebSocket Context               | One WebSocket session                      |


🔍 In Short
The life span of a dependency depends on its scope — how it is defined in the framework (like Spring) and how long it is supposed to live in memory.


👉 @Scope is used at the class level, not method level.

🔍 What does @Scope do?
The @Scope annotation in Spring tells the container how long the bean should live, and how it should be created or reused.

✅ Correct usage (on a class):
java
Copy
Edit
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class MyBean {
    public MyBean() {
        System.out.println("New instance created!");
    }
}
Every time this bean is injected or requested — a new instance will be created because it's prototype.

❌ Invalid usage (on a method):
java
Copy
Edit
public class MyConfig {
    @Scope("prototype") // ❌ This has no effect here
    public MyBean myBean() {
        return new MyBean();
    }
}
The above won’t work unless you use @Bean along with @Scope in a configuration class:

✅ Valid usage with @Bean (in config class):
java
Copy
Edit
@Configuration
public class MyConfig {

    @Bean
    @Scope("prototype") // ✅ Valid on @Bean methods
    public MyBean myBean() {
        return new MyBean();
    }
}
✅ Summary
| Where to Use `@Scope`    | Valid? | Notes                                     |
| ------------------------ | ------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| On a `@Component` class  | ✅      | Common usage                              |
| On a method with `@Bean` | ✅      | Valid when used in `@Configuration` class |
| On a regular method      | ❌      | Has no effect                             |
